{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at 0c5764abbc6d31d5dca55b79a1cf1b180c9aac7d. All 17 artifacts inspected. The native-container feasibility result and technology recommendation are supported; these corrections concern the meaning of the evidence, not schedule or functional scope.\n\nP1 — The comparison Worker can merge the wrong candidate during concurrent requests (spikes/sandbox-git/src/iso.ts:34, 69–80, 102–108; note:190–192). Cached requests share spike-base, spike-head and spike-land without serialization. A can fetch candidate A, pause at merge, B overwrite spike-head, and A merge B while its response still says headSha=A. I reproduced this using the exact op source, real isomorphic-git merge/object creation and MemoryFS, with only network fetch/push mocked: A response head efc4a022… but its generated commit's second parent e1ad4ef7… was B. Repro: /tmp/artroom-checker-0c5764ab/spikes/sandbox-git/.checker-race.mjs. One remote winner and equality to the returned commit do not prove that winner contains its requested candidate. Correct the comparison with per-request immutable OIDs/refs and isolated mutation or per-repo serialization; add a deterministic same-isolate test that checks actual parents/tree against the requested candidate. Qualify the existing 10 races as observations of ref acceptance, removing the claim that the contingency's races are safe until corrected evidence exists. No production fallback build is required to resolve this.\n\nP2 — A failed push is not necessarily a lease refusal (src/index.ts:224–226; src/iso.ts:109–113). Every nonzero native push is returned as landed:false/refused:lease, including authentication, transport and lost-response failures. In the last case the receiver may already have accepted the commit. Keep a definite stale-ref/lease rejection distinct from an error or unknown outcome; return the pinned commit/expected base and document that an unknown outcome needs readback/reconciliation before another land. The spike need not implement the Room's full durable recovery now. A local lost-response/transport classification check is sufficient here; the delayed authenticated-push recovery test remains Lane B work.\n\nArchitecture: approve native Git as the preferred merge/publish engine based on 80 measured operations and 20 native races. The recommendation must retain the accepted immutable reservation, scoped credentials and uncertain-push recovery when adopted in the Room. The comparison does not yet establish candidate integrity under same-isolate concurrency.\n\nSecurity: token injection outside the container, explicit lease and shared-key spike boundary are sensible. Recorded raw results contain no literal art_v tokens. This is trusted spike code, not an adversarial security demonstration; gateway redirects, strict endpoint/method checks, operation-scoped capability disposal, output bounds and process deadlines remain product validation work. No additional production hardening is demanded by this spike review.\n\nSimplification: one native Git engine is a sound default and avoids maintaining two merge semantics. Keep the alternative as a documented contingency without shrinking the full functional scope.\n\nValidation: npm ci with scripts disabled; both Wrangler configurations bundle with deploy --dry-run; independently recomputed all 15 benchmark groups from raw rows (10 successful operations each); 10/10 native and 10/10 comparison sequential lands; recorded 20 native and 10 comparison races each report one winner and main==returned commit; actual local concurrent-merge reproduction demonstrates why that final comparison predicate is insufficient. No live deployment, remote mutation or credential access by checker.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5208479efbf2b56b0de1f7e33f7f5f1cd6327355","binding":"assigned","head":"0c5764abbc6d31d5dca55b79a1cf1b180c9aac7d","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f75723